From: ARMDeck <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8250-oneplus-instantnoodle: restore the removed_mem reservation

The board DTS deletes removed_mem from sm8250.dtsi ("removed_mem @
0x80b00000 is bigger so we gotta shift everything up") and moves the
firmware carve-outs up to 0x8dc00000, but never adds removed_mem back.
The bootloader's /memory node covers 0x80000000-0xb98fffff, so Linux used
0x80b00000-0x8dbfffff (about 210 MB) as ordinary RAM.

That range belongs to the secure world. The pages sit in ZONE_DMA32, which
the allocator only falls back to once the higher zones are full, so the
phone ran fine until RAM filled up (large downloads filling the page cache,
or any big allocation). Then the first write there reset the phone
instantly: PMIC warm reset through PS_HOLD, nothing in the kernel log, even
when streamed live over the network.

Reproduced on a OnePlus 8 (IN2013, OxygenOS 13.1 firmware) on 2026-10-02:
- stress-ng --vm 4 --vm-bytes 8000M: reset within 5 s; 2000M: no reset.
- 8 parallel copies of a 1.5 GB file to UFS (~330 MB/s): reset in 5-20 s,
  as soon as MemFree reached the bottom. Ruled out first: UFS power
  management, UFS queue depth, the USB cable/charging path, S8C voltage.
With this patch both run for 2-3 minutes with MemFree at 150-350 MB
(72 GB written in 3 minutes, stress-ng --verify clean), no reset.

Size: 0xcd00000, as in the two other OnePlus 8 device trees
(WuerfelDev/linux-sm8250, ObiKeahloa/linux). It is a superset of the
removed_region sizes in OnePlus's own trees (0xAF00000 in
OnePlusOSS/android_kernel_oneplus_sm8250 R_11.0 kona.dtsi, 0x5300000 in
LineageOS lineage-23.2 kona.dtsi and mainline sm8250.dtsi), and ends below
camera_mem at 0x8dc00000. Reserving the larger range only costs RAM.
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-instantnoodle.dts	2026-10-02 11:17:43.062673999 +0300
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-instantnoodle.dts	2026-10-02 11:17:43.082981829 +0300
@@ -178,6 +178,11 @@
 	};
 
 	reserved-memory {
+		removed_mem: memory@80b00000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0x80b00000 0x0 0xcd00000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
 		camera_mem: camera@8dc00000 {
 			reg = <0x0 0x8dc00000 0x0 0x500000>;
 			no-map;
